(The widening is earned, not assumed. A list stays on the homogeneous fast
 path when the function it is handed to only reads it, and when the function
 appends the very type the caller has already proven the list holds - the
 call edge carries the callee's write into the caller's proof, and a write
 that agrees with the proof is no widening at all. docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'report the size' with a list called tally.
  Print "size: {tally's length}".

To 'add one more' with a list called tally and a number called extra.
  append extra to tally.

To 'note whatever' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.

(Read only: nothing is written, so nothing widens.)
a list called counted is [1, 2, 3].
'report the size' of counted.
Print "counted last: {counted's last}".

(Written with the type the caller proved: still one type, still homogeneous.)
a list called measured is [10, 20].
'add one more' of measured and 30.
Print "measured last: {measured's last}".
Print "measured: {measured}".

(A list of texts handed to a function that appends a text keeps its proof
 the same way.)
a list called guests is ["ann"].
'note whatever' of guests and "bea".
Print "guests last: {guests's last}".
